# ============================================================================
# justpae — environment variables
#
# Copy to .env.local for local development, and set the same values in the
# hosting provider's project settings for deployment. .env.local is gitignored;
# NEVER commit real values.
#
# Anything prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_ is compiled into the browser bundle and is
# therefore PUBLIC. Only the Supabase URL, the anon key (which is designed to
# be public and is governed entirely by RLS) and the feature flags carry that
# prefix. Every credential below is server-only.
#
# ── Moving to a new Supabase project (new account, empty project) ──────────
# Code and migrations don't change — only these three values do, in Vercel:
#   1. NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL
#   2. NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY
#   3. SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY
# Plus, once per new project, all dashboard-only (see
# supabase/email-templates/README.md for the full checklist):
#   - Authentication → Providers → Email → "Confirm email": ON
#   - Paste both email templates
#   - Authentication → Emails → SMTP Settings: custom SMTP configured
#   - Authentication → URL Configuration: Site URL = https://justpae.com,
#     Redirect URLs include https://justpae.com/auth/callback and
#     https://justpae.com/auth/confirm
#   - Authentication → Providers → Google: client ID/secret entered, with
#     https://<new-project-ref>.supabase.co/auth/v1/callback added as an
#     Authorized redirect URI in the Google Cloud OAuth client — this is a
#     Supabase-side callback, not the app's own domain, so it changes with
#     every new project regardless of APP_URL.
# ============================================================================

# ── Supabase ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Project URL and anon key: dashboard → Project Settings → API.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key

# Service role key. BYPASSES RLS COMPLETELY. Used only by the webhook
# receivers, the cron reconcilers, the automated-payout path and the
# admin-gated server actions. If this ever appears in a client bundle, every
# user's data is readable by anyone — it has no NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix for that
# reason and must never be given one.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your-service-role-key

# Google sign-in, for `supabase start` locally. In a deployed project these are
# set in the dashboard under Authentication → Providers → Google instead.
SUPABASE_AUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
SUPABASE_AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=

# ── App ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The deployed origin, no trailing slash. Used to build auth email links, the
# OAuth redirect and the /receive share link. A wrong value here produces
# confirmation emails whose links point at the wrong host — set it per
# environment, including preview deployments.
APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# BOOTSTRAP ONLY, as of Phase 4. Admin IS a database role now — the
# admin_users table added in migration 0019 is the authority, and grants and
# revocations are recorded in the activity ledger.
#
# What this list still does: the first time an address in it signs in, it
# creates its own admin_users row as 'superadmin', marked `bootstrapped` so the
# ledger never claims a human granted it. That exists so a fresh project has a
# first admin without anyone hand-writing a row in the dashboard SQL editor.
#
# What it does NOT do: resurrect a revoked admin. An admin removed through
# /admin/users stays removed even if their address is still sitting here, so
# revoking access does not also require an env edit and a redeploy.
#
# Every admin page and action additionally requires aal2 — a session that has
# presented a TOTP code. Turn MFA on in the Supabase dashboard (Authentication
# → Multi-Factor) or nobody can enrol and the admin area is unreachable.
ADMIN_EMAILS=you@example.com

# ── Email (transactional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Resend API key. Used by src/lib/email.ts for the app's own transactional
# email — PIN lockouts, payout recipient changes, and (from Phase 3) KYC and
# money-movement notifications. Separate from the Supabase AUTH emails, which
# go through the SMTP settings configured in the Supabase dashboard.
#
# OPTIONAL and FAILS SOFT. With this unset, sends are skipped with a warn log
# and nothing else breaks: email is a notification channel, never a
# prerequisite. A withdrawal that completed must not report failure because an
# email provider was down, and a PIN that locked must stay locked.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# The From address. MUST be on a domain verified with Resend — justpae.com. An
# address on an unverified domain is silently dropped or spam-foldered, which
# looks exactly like broken email code.
EMAIL_FROM=justpae <no-reply@justpae.com>

# Comma-separated. Where operational alerts go (KYC submissions, from Phase 3).
ADMIN_NOTIFICATION_EMAILS=

# The company's real registered mailing address, shown in every email's
# footer. OPTIONAL and FAILS SOFT — layout.ts omits the line entirely rather
# than invent one — but several jurisdictions this product operates in
# require a real postal address on commercial email, so set this before
# going live. Plain-text sibling emails:build generates needs it set in the
# shell too, not just Vercel, since that script is plain Node and does not
# read .env.local.
COMPANY_ADDRESS=

# ── Cron ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Shared secret for the reconciliation routes, sent as
# `Authorization: Bearer <value>`.
#
# REQUIRED. The gate FAILS CLOSED: with this unset every cron route returns 503
# rather than running. Those routes credit deposits and complete payouts, so an
# unset secret must not leave them publicly callable.
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
CRON_SECRET=

# ── Busha (primary provider: NGN/KES/USDT deposits, conversions, payouts) ───
# Secret key from the Busha Business dashboard.
#
# Note: a real registered Business account's key was rejected outright by the
# documented sandbox host and worked immediately against production. Do not
# assume a sandbox exists for your account before testing it.
BUSHA_API_KEY=
BUSHA_API_BASE_URL=https://api.busha.io

# Webhook signing secret. The receiver expects `x-bu-signature` carrying
# base64(HMAC-SHA256(secret, rawBody)).
BUSHA_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Reject deliveries whose signature doesn't match, instead of logging and
# continuing. Left OFF by default on purpose: the header name and scheme were
# established by reverse-engineering real deliveries rather than from published
# documentation, and a wrong guess here rejects every genuine webhook with a
# 401 before it is even recorded — which is exactly what happened on the
# predecessor of this code and left months of deposits uncredited. Turn it on
# once real traffic has passed with no logged mismatch.
#
# Safe to leave off: the receiver never acts on the payload's own claims. It
# re-fetches the transfer from the provider's API and acts only on that.
BUSHA_WEBHOOK_REJECT_INVALID_SIGNATURE=false

# ── Klasha (GHS deposit collection only) ────────────────────────────────────
# Behind NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_KLASHA_GHS_DEPOSIT, which ships off. Every live
# call so far has returned an account-wide 403, including a bodyless GET, so
# the account itself is not cleared. Leave these blank until it is.
KLASHA_LOGIN_EMAIL=
KLASHA_LOGIN_PASSWORD=
KLASHA_PUBLIC_KEY=
# Documented as a 3DES key, but the real secret base64-decodes to 32 bytes — a
# valid AES-256 length and an invalid 3DES one. Try AES-256-CBC first once
# account access clears.
KLASHA_ENCRYPTION_SECRET=
KLASHA_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
KLASHA_API_BASE_URL=https://dev.kcookery.com

# ── Korapay (NGN virtual accounts, NGN/KES payouts, treasury sweep) ─────────
# Merchant API, https://api.korapay.com/merchant/api/v1. Unlike Busha, Korapay
# has a REAL sandbox: test keys are sk_test_/pk_test_ prefixed and hit the same
# host, so there is no separate base URL to configure — KORAPAY_API_BASE_URL
# exists only so a future self-hosted mock can be pointed at.
#
# NEVER COMMIT A LIVE KEY, and never let one near a test run. A key beginning
# sk_live_ moves real money on the first call; the sandbox prefix is the only
# thing that makes the probe script and the e2e exercises safe to run at all.
KORAPAY_SECRET_KEY=
KORAPAY_PUBLIC_KEY=
KORAPAY_API_BASE_URL=https://api.korapay.com/merchant/api/v1
# The bank the permanent virtual accounts are issued against. "000" is the
# sandbox's mock bank; the live code is whichever partner bank Korapay
# provisions for this merchant and MUST be confirmed with them before
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_KORAPAY_NGN_DEPOSIT goes on — a wrong code here issues
# account numbers at a bank that will never receive the customer's transfer.
KORAPAY_VBA_BANK_CODE=000

# ── USD collection (Feature 1, /receive) ────────────────────────────────────
# No provider is confirmed yet, so there is no credential to set. When one is
# chosen, add its variables here and implement the interface in
# src/lib/providers/usdCollection.ts.
# USD_COLLECTION_API_KEY=

# ── Bills provider (Feature 5) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# No provider is chosen yet. See the header of src/lib/providers/bills.ts for
# the realistic options covering Nigeria, Ghana and Kenya.
# BILLS_API_KEY=
# BILLS_API_SECRET=

# ── Feature flags ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# All default OFF. Each gates a path whose provider is not yet confirmed for
# this account. The rule: when a provider is unconfirmed, the UI, schema and
# server action are all built, the provider call sits behind an interface, and
# the flag ships off with an honest "coming soon" state. Flipping the flag is
# then the only change needed once credentials land.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ because a Client Component renders the right state without a
# round trip. These say "is this feature on", never "here is a secret".

# Feature 1 — USD receiving details on /receive.
# Waiting on: which provider will issue USD collection on this account.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_USD_COLLECTION=false

# Feature 2 — the whole GHS wallet (deposit via Klasha, convert, withdraw,
# Pay to China funding). Waiting on: Klasha account access (every live call
# returns a 403), the real request-body encryption scheme, and the webhook
# signature scheme.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_GHS_WALLET=false

# Feature 5 — bills and airtime.
# Waiting on: provider selection.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_BILLS=false

# Feature 6 — flight booking.
# Waiting on: provider verification.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_FLIGHTS=false

# Feature 7 — the USD wallet (deposit, convert, withdraw, Pay to China
# funding). Waiting on: a decision to turn it on — there is no USD deposit
# rail; it is funded by Receive USD (Feature 1) or by converting in.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_USD_WALLET=false

# Korapay — three rails, all off by default.
#
# KORAPAY_NGN_DEPOSIT replaces the manual naira deposit for customers when on;
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_NGN_MANUAL_DEPOSIT stays the fallback the moment it goes
# off, so there is never a window with no naira deposit path.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_KORAPAY_NGN_DEPOSIT=false
# The master switch for Korapay payouts. WHICH currencies actually use it is
# the per-currency setting in the admin area (Rates and markups → Payout
# routing); Busha is the default for every currency and both have to agree.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_KORAPAY_PAYOUTS=false
# The daily automatic treasury sweep. The only flag here that moves company
# money with nobody pressing anything — leave it off until the Busha funding
# account has been resolved and confirmed in the admin area.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_KORAPAY_AUTO_SWEEP=false

# GROUP 7 — kill switches, not feature flags. NGN and KES bank deposit are
# LIVE today; these default to false meaning "not in maintenance" so leaving
# them unset changes nothing. Set to true only to deliberately take one rail
# down (a provider outage) without affecting the other, USDT, or the rest of
# that currency's wallet. See src/lib/flags.ts.
NEXT_PUBLIC_MAINTENANCE_NGN_BANK_DEPOSIT=false
NEXT_PUBLIC_MAINTENANCE_KES_BANK_DEPOSIT=false

# GROUP 4 — naira deposits by manual bank transfer. ON WHEN UNSET, which is
# the opposite of every feature flag above and deliberate: the provider's
# temporary naira accounts are unavailable, so the rail this replaces is down.
# Unset meaning "off" would ship a maintenance notice and no way to deposit
# naira at all. Set it to "false" to take manual naira deposits down; the
# admin queue stays reachable either way, because requests already in it still
# need answering.
#
# Set the receiving account and the limits in the admin area (Rates and
# markups → Naira deposit account), NOT here — the customer screen refuses to
# show instructions until the bank, number and name are all set.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_NGN_MANUAL_DEPOSIT=true

# ── Analytics ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Google Analytics measurement ID, e.g. G-XXXXXXXXXX.
#
# OPTIONAL, and OFF when unset — no script tag is rendered at all, not a
# disabled one. An analytics tag that ships enabled is a tracker nobody decided
# to add.
#
# PUBLIC PAGES ONLY. It loads on the landing page and the two legal pages and
# is never rendered by the dashboard layout. Behind sign-in a URL is not
# anonymous — /transactions?type=withdrawal alongside a session says something
# about an identified person's money — and sending that to an advertising
# company is not something a payments product should do quietly.
#
# TURNING IT ON TAKES TWO CHANGES, deliberately: this variable, AND adding
# googletagmanager.com to script-src in securityHeaders() in next.config.ts.
# The CSP is 'self' only, so setting this alone loads nothing.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID=
